Found five release-significant issues. No files were changed.

- [P1] Asset GC can delete live application and project state. The protected allowlist contains only `workflows` and `TRASH`; everything else under `output/` is treated as an artifact candidate ([asset_gc.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/asset_gc.py:20), [asset_gc.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/asset_gc.py:144)). On the current workspace, dry-run classified 2,695 files as orphans, including 152 control JSON files such as `pipeline/*/pipeline.json`, `projects/*/initial.json`, and `music_history.json`. The UI then preselects every result and offers permanent deletion after one confirmation ([AssetGarbageCollection.vue](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/components/AssetGarbageCollection.vue:27)). This can erase editable projects and pipeline history even though the dialog claims workflow state is excluded. Restrict scanning to explicitly managed artifact roots, or protect every control/state namespace.

- [P1] GC races active executions and can delete newly produced artifacts. Collection performs an unlocked reference scan followed by deletion ([asset_gc.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/asset_gc.py:168)). Meanwhile, the scheduler promotes a node’s staged files before persisting its `artifact_refs` ([scheduler.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/scheduler.py:728)). During that window—and for legacy adapters writing directly to final directories—the file appears orphaned. GC needs coordination with active project/execution locks or must conservatively exclude active project paths.

- [P2] Restore rewrites arbitrary user content, not just references. `_rewrite()` runs unrestricted `str.replace()` over every string in workflows and execution records ([project_archive.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/project_archive.py:242)). Consequently, script text, descriptions, URLs, logs, or payloads containing an old workflow/project/execution ID are silently altered. Rewrite only schema-defined identity and managed-path fields.

- [P2] Crafted archives can write “artifacts” into protected workflow state. Restore accepts any lexically safe path after `artifacts/`, then joins it directly beneath `output/` ([project_archive.py](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/project_archive.py:378)). An archive can therefore contain paths such as `artifacts/workflows/queue/...`, `workflows/hook-tokens/...`, or other control namespaces. Existing symlinked parent directories are also not rejected. Enforce an artifact-root allowlist and verify every resolved destination remains beneath a non-symlink managed artifact directory.

- [P2] Step 9.5’s performance acceptance criterion is not actually measured. The benchmark times only `createCanvasNodeProjector()` in Node, excluding Vue rendering, Vue Flow layout, DOM paint, culling, and pointer interaction ([benchmark-large-canvas.mjs](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/scripts/benchmark-large-canvas.mjs:15)). The interaction test replaces Vue Flow with a lightweight mock ([largeCanvasPerformance.test.js](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/workflow/__tests__/largeCanvasPerformance.test.js:21)). The documentation merely provides instructions for a future visual spot check, so “loads, pans, and drags without dropped-frame stalls” remains unverified.

Verification completed: 34 focused backend tests and 8 focused frontend tests pass, confirming these are coverage/design gaps rather than existing test failures.
